Figure  14 b reveals the trend of the peak shift depending on the summed ring number of three rings for C1s and N1s XPS spectra. The peak positions of calculated C1s XPS spectra obtained in this work were plotted against the total number of three rings surrounding one analyzing carbon atom in Fig.  14 b. Similarly, the peak positions of N1s XPS spectra for tertiary nitrogen from our previous work [ 122 ] were plotted against the total number of three rings surrounding one nitrogen atom in Fig.  14 b. For example, a summed ring number of 18 indicates one analyzed carbon atom in the middle of three hexagonal rings (6 × 3 = 18) or one carbon atom in the middle of one pentagon, one hexagon, and one heptagon (5 + 6 + 7 = 18). Our previous work for N1s XPS spectra showed the tendency of the shift depending on the number of non-hexagonal rings but the work just considered the type of two rings surrounding the analyzed atom [ 122 ]. Also, our previous work showed the peak shift of C=C in C1s XPS spectra depending on the types of non-hexagonal rings [ 94 ,  115 ,  117 ], but only two kinds of rings such as pentagon-hexagon or heptagon-hexagon were discussed at the same time. In addition, the peak position of pentagons differs depending on the number of the surrounding hexagons [ 81 ], but a detailed discussion regarding the peak positions of carbon/nitrogen surrounded by three rings was not conducted because the relationship was unclear at that time. In contrast, this work clearly shows that the summed ring number of three rings surrounding the analyzed atom correlates with the peak shift. From these results, the extrapolated lines (Eqs.  2  and  3  in Fig.  14 b) can be used to estimate the peak positions of unreported defects. For example, the peak positions of carbon atoms surrounded by 5–5–6 rings and 6–7–8 rings in graphene are estimated to be 283.0 and 285.9 eV, respectively, using Eq.  2 . 2 For C1s , y = 0 . 57x + 273.9 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
